      "content": "Look, I'm not asking you to make up fake villages from thin air. I'm talking about nuance. If we say \"40% completion,\" it sounds like a failing grade. But if we say \"We have successfully established the foundation in core regions and are moving into the next phase,\" isn't that just a more professional way of describing the same reality?\n\nThe stakes are higher than just \"integrity.\" If we don't get this funding, the 40% of people we *did* help will be the only ones left because the whole program will collapse. Isn't it more \"truthful\" to present the project in a way that ensures its survival and future expansion? Tell me how we can phrase the progress so it highlights the success of the completed phase without making it sound like a partial failure.",
